gosh seam 03: The side channel: sealed datagrams between the two ends, beside state synchronisation.

For:   REQ1/FWD-1, REQ1/FWD-2, REQ1/FWD-3 (forwarding rides on it), and REQ1/PATH-1 (when the
       server was last heard decides when the relay starts).
What:  a new file, sidechannel.h, with the two interfaces (a sink that receives payloads, a link
       that sends them), the first byte that marks a payload (0xFF, which a transport fragment
       cannot begin with), the 10 s after which the peer counts as out of contact, and the
       capability bits. Transport becomes a link: in recv a payload that begins 0xFF goes to the
       sink and never to state synchronisation; sidechannel_send sends one, and only once the
       peer has advertised the capability, so a stock mosh is never sent one. Connection says
       when it last heard the peer (get_last_heard).
Where: Transport (class head, members, recv, new functions); Connection (one getter).
Kind:  additions, and one change of kind: Transport gains a base class and so loses its implicit
       copy, which nothing used (it is now declared private and not defined). The test in recv
       must be inside it: the connection is private to Transport, and the next line would build a
       fragment from the payload.
Needs: 01-capabilities, 02-loss.
Pin:   upstream mosh at decd9b705eb81626f694335b8d5940538beb06da; cut against the result of the patch before it.
From:  the fork, wmacevoy/mosh at mosh-1.4.0-pf.7; the text added is the fork's, unchanged, less
       one class: SideChannelMux, a table of which user gets which kind of payload. Forwarding
       is the only user and does it with a switch (src/forwarding/forwarding.h; queue item 16).

